  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nIt's another beautiful July day as a broad surface high\nstretches across much of the Great Lakes region. Increasing \nsoutherly flow on its western periphery will support a gradual \nwarming trend heading into the weekend. Highs are expected to \npeak on Saturday through Monday with widespread 90s likely each \nday. Heat impacts will be a concern, especially with dewpoint \ntemperatures ranging between 65 to 75 degrees. This will result \nin afternoon heat indices approaching 100F each day. The heat \ncontinues into the week, though with the ridge expected to \nweaken and a passing shortwave or two in the flow aloft, we \nshould see some relief from the heat. It may not be much, but it\nwill feel better than nothing.\n\nOur rain chances will remain limited over the next 7 days,\nthough there is an increasing signal for at least some \nscattered storms Sunday PM. A very moist and unstable airmass \nwill be in place across the region as a subtle impulse in the \nflow spreads east from the Dakotas. For any storms that develop \nwithin this forcing, it will be a prime environment for severe \nweather given the increasing LLJ and several thousand J/kg of \nMUCAPE. There is some uncertainty with timing as well, but late \nafternoon into the evening would be favored as the EML deepens. \nA 15% risk for severe weather is centered over MN and WI for the\nDay 4 SWO.\n\nIt should be highlighted as well that there is a Day 2 Slight\nRisk across portions of northern MN and eastern ND. There are \nsome indications that decaying storms could continue into \ncentral MN Friday evening, though instability quickly decreases \nthe further south the storms travel which should limit their \nmobility.\n\nUnder this building ridge, northwesterly flow aloft may usher in \nperiods of elevated smoke from wildfires across western North \nAmerica. The impacts from this would be more if higher \nconcentrations were expected to be at the surface, but it is \nworth noting since it could still be noticeable in the sky at \nthe very least.",
